# Copy to `.env.local` (that file is gitignored) and fill in.

# ── Encryption key for stored secrets ───────────────────────────────────────
# Required before an admin can save any API key or partner payout account from
# the admin panel — those values are encrypted at rest with it (AES-256-GCM,
# lib/security/secretBox.ts). Nothing else needs it: without it the app runs
# fine on env-var keys alone, /admin/ai-settings just refuses to store one.
#
# LOSING THIS MAKES EVERY STORED SECRET UNREADABLE. It is not derivable from
# anything else. Keep it in the deployment's env (Railway) and nowhere in git.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
SECRETS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ── AI providers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every key below is now also settable from /admin/ai-settings, which stores it
# encrypted in the DB and takes precedence over the env var. These stay the
# fallback, and are still the simplest way to configure a fresh deployment.
#
# Profile extraction — the AI interview will not run without at least one of
# these four. Every AI feature defaults to Claude; an admin can switch any
# feature to another provider from /admin/ai-settings once its key is set.
# Get one at https://platform.claude.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Optional — only needed for features an admin has switched to ChatGPT.
# Get one at https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional — only needed for features an admin has switched to Gemini.
# Get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional — only needed for features an admin has switched to DeepSeek.
# Cheapest of the four, but text-only (no photo/PDF biodata) and its JSON
# mode has no schema enforcement — see lib/ai/providers/deepseek.ts.
# Get one at https://platform.deepseek.com/api_keys
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=

# Optional — real TTS/STT for the voice interview (lib/speech/). While empty,
# voice runs on the browser's own free Web Speech API — works, but robotic and
# patchy on Hindi/Hinglish. Sarvam AI is built specifically for Hinglish
# code-mixing and keeps audio in India; swapping in webSpeech.ts and
# webSpeechOutput.ts is meant to be a config change, nothing above the
# SpeechProvider/SpeechOutputProvider interface needs to know which is running.
# Get one at https://dashboard.sarvam.ai (₹1000 free credit on signup).
SARVAM_API_KEY=

# "mock" (default) serves the mock view-model providers in lib/data/.
# "api" is real now — Postgres-backed via Prisma (see docker-compose.yml).
NEXT_PUBLIC_DATA_MODE=mock

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local Postgres, per docker-compose.yml (docker compose up -d).
#
# In production this is a managed Postgres with the `vector` extension
# available (prisma/schema.prisma declares it). Supabase, Neon and AWS RDS all
# provide it. Nothing in the app knows which one it is — moving between them is
# this one line, which is the whole point of not keeping data on the app host.
#
# Supabase: Project Settings -> Database -> Connection string -> URI.
# Use the **Session pooler** (port 5432) rather than the direct connection —
# a container host opens and drops connections per deploy, and the direct
# endpoint runs out of slots long before the pooler does.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://bandhantak:bandhantak_dev@localhost:15432/bandhantak?schema=public

# ── Object storage (photos, voice notes) ────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. Without it, uploads go to the container filesystem
# and are lost on the next deploy — the app boots with a warning saying so
# (see lib/services/storage/objectStore.ts).
#
# Any S3-compatible store works, unchanged: Cloudflare R2, AWS S3, Supabase
# Storage, Backblaze B2, DigitalOcean Spaces, MinIO. That is deliberate — it
# makes the choice of vendor four variables rather than a rewrite.
#
# Cloudflare R2 (recommended: egress is free, and photos are read far more
# often than they are written):
#   S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   S3_REGION=auto
# AWS S3:
#   S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com
#   S3_REGION=<region>
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_REGION=auto
S3_BUCKET=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Where public objects are read from — a custom domain or the bucket's public
# subdomain, NOT the API endpoint above. Profile photos are served straight
# from here by <img src>; gated media never is (it goes through
# /api/media/[id], which re-checks permission per request).
#
# The bucket must allow public reads on the `photos/` prefix and MUST NOT on
# `media/`.
S3_PUBLIC_URL=

# Signs session JWTs (D-30). Generate your own for real deployments:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('hex'))"
JWT_SECRET=

# Subscriptions (D-10/M09). While these three are empty, checkout runs on a
# built-in dummy gateway (/checkout/dummy) — real money never moves, and the
# whole flow (order → webhook → subscription → commission) is fully testable
# without them. Fill all three in together to switch to real Razorpay; no code
# change needed. Get keys at https://dashboard.razorpay.com/app/keys and set
# the webhook secret when adding the webhook URL (…/api/webhooks/payments) in
# Razorpay's dashboard.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Google Sign-In. While these two are empty the "Continue with Google" button
# simply doesn't render — the mobile/email + password flow is unaffected.
# Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID (type: Web application) at
# https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials and add these two
# Authorized redirect URIs, exactly:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback
#   https://<your-domain>/api/auth/google/callback
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Web Push (browser notifications). While these are empty, notices still land
# in /user/inbox and the header bell — only the lock-screen buzz is missing,
# and the opt-in card hides itself rather than offering something that can't
# work. Generate the pair once with:
#   npx tsx scripts/generate-vapid-keys.ts
# Generate it ONCE per deployment: the public key is baked into every browser
# subscription handed out so far, so regenerating silently kills push for every
# user who already opted in.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Push services require a contact for the sending server (RFC 8292 §2.1).
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:support@bandhantak.com

# ── Public URL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The origin used to build links that leave the app — partner invite/lead
# links, outreach WhatsApp and email bodies. Falls back to
# https://bandhantak.com, so a wrong value here sends people to the wrong host
# rather than breaking anything. APP_URL is the server-side fallback if the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ one isn't set.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Scheduled jobs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The three /api/cron/* endpoints (ops, lifecycle, partner-outreach) refuse to
# run at all while this is empty — they message real people, refund buyers and
# release partner earnings, so an unauthenticated version of them must not
# exist. Set it, then point a scheduler at each route:
#   curl -X POST https://<host>/api/cron/lifecycle \
#     -H "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET"
# `ops` is HOURLY and is the one that is not optional — it carries the booking
# deadlines the product has already promised in writing. See DEPLOYMENT.md §5.
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
CRON_SECRET=

# ── Partner outreach (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# While empty, outreach falls back to opening WhatsApp/email on the partner's
# own device — nothing is sent from the server. RESEND_API_KEY and
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN are also settable from /admin/ai-settings (encrypted);
# the rest of the WhatsApp config stays here.
# Email: https://resend.com/api-keys
RESEND_API_KEY=
OUTREACH_EMAIL_FROM=
OUTREACH_EMAIL_REPLY_TO=
# WhatsApp Cloud API: https://developers.facebook.com/apps → WhatsApp → API Setup.
# Without WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NAME, sending only works inside a 24-hour session
# window; with it, an approved template is used instead.
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_LANG=hi
WHATSAPP_API_VERSION=v21.0

# ── Partner payouts (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# While empty, an approved withdrawal is transferred by hand from your own bank
# and the admin records the UTR — see lib/services/payouts/. Filling all three
# in switches on automatic RazorpayX transfers.
RAZORPAYX_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAYX_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAYX_ACCOUNT_NUMBER=

# ── Contact verification (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# /user/verify-contact — phone via Twilio Verify, email via a dedicated Resend
# sender. Either half missing → that channel's send returns a clear
# "not configured" result rather than failing; login/registration never block
# on it. See lib/services/verification/contactVerification/.
# Twilio Verify: https://console.twilio.com/us1/develop/verify/services
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_VERIFY_SERVICE_SID=
# Reuses RESEND_API_KEY above (same Resend account, different from-address —
# outreach and OTP mail are deliberately not the same sender identity).
AUTH_EMAIL_FROM=
